{
  "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0306987714002722",
  "title": "Pre-eclampsia renamed and reframed: Intra-abdominal hypertension in pregnancy",
  "content": "Citation and publication\n- Title: \"Pre-eclampsia renamed and reframed: Intra-abdominal hypertension in pregnancy\"\n- Authors: Diane J. Sawchuck, Bernd K. Wittmann\n- Journal: Medical Hypotheses (Elsevier). Volume 83, Issue 5, November 2014, pages 619\u2013632\n- DOI: 10.1016/j.mehy.2014.08.001\n- Open access: Creative Commons BY-NC-ND 3.0\n- Article type: Research hypothesis\n- Metrics (page): Cited by 35; Mendeley readers 131\n\nCore hypothesis and key equation\n- Hypothesis: Pre-eclampsia is caused by intra-abdominal hypertension in pregnancy (IAH-P). Sustained or rising intra-abdominal pressure (IAP) \u2265 12 mmHg initiates a cascade leading to multi-organ dysfunction that presents as pre-eclampsia.\n- Central adapted equation: \u0394IAP-P = \u0394IAVF / C_ab\n  - \u0394IAP-P = change in intra-abdominal pressure in pregnancy\n  - \u0394IAVF = change in intra-abdominal vector force (volume and force direction)\n  - C_ab = abdominal compliance\n\nMechanisms and pathophysiology (main steps)\n- Elevated IAP (\u226512 mmHg) causes:\n  - Impaired venous return to the heart\n  - Increased systemic vascular resistance\n  - Ischemia\u2013reperfusion injury\n  - Increased intestinal permeability \u2192 translocation of lipopolysaccharide (LPS, endotoxin) to liver\n  - Cytotoxic immune responses and systemic inflammatory response (SIRS)\n  - Pressure transmission to thoracic and intracranial compartments\n  - Multi-organ dysfunction (clinical pre-eclampsia manifestations)\n- The hypothesis is grounded in Pascal\u2019s law and published intra-abdominal hypertension (IAH) literature.\n\nFactors increasing intra-abdominal pressure in pregnancy (listed)\n- Progressive uterine expansion over gestation\n- Obstetrical factors causing excessive or acute increases in intra-uterine volume\n- Maternal anthropometrics that alter IAP thresholds (e.g., BMI, abdominal wall)\n- Maternal postures that change abdominal force direction\n- Decreased abdominal compliance (C_ab), diminished with advancing gestation or when maximum expansion reached\n- Environmental factors: habitation at high altitude, rapid drops in barometric pressure\n\nMicrobiome and endotoxin translocation\n- Authors postulate threshold for LPS translocation depends on:\n  - Magnitude of IAP\n  - Intestinal microbiome composition\n  - Degree of intestinal barrier permeability\n- LPS translocation to the liver is proposed as a key trigger of systemic inflammation in this model.\n\nClinical implication / conclusion\n- Delivery acts as abdominal decompression and, according to the hypothesis, cures pre-eclampsia by reducing IAP.\n- The work reframes pre-eclampsia as potentially explainable by physical pressure dynamics (IAH-P) interacting with intestinal permeability and immunologic responses.\n\nArticle structure and supporting items\n- Major sections: Abstract; Hypothesis; Causes of increased IAP in pregnancy; IAH-P causal pathway and regression equation; Current knowledge of IAH/ACS and IAP in pregnancy; Microbiome/intestine/LPS; Discussion on renaming pre-eclampsia; Summary; References.\n- Figures: Fig.1 Etiology pathway for IAH-P (pre-eclampsia); Fig.2 Mean intra-abdominal pressure by position and gestational age.\n- Tables: Table 1 \u2014 laws of physics and formulas relevant to the hypothesis; Table 2 \u2014 mean intra-abdominal pressure measurement by position.\n\nAbbreviations (selected key)\n- IAH: intra-abdominal hypertension; IAH-P: intra-abdominal hypertension in pregnancy; IAP: intra-abdominal pressure; C_ab: abdominal compliance; LPS: lipopolysaccharide; SIRS: systemic inflammatory response; HELLP, DIC, ACS, BMI as standard pregnancy/critical care abbreviations used in the text.\n\nFunding / conflicts\n- Article includes authorship contributions, sources of funding support, and conflict of interest statement (details within article).\n\nPrimary conclusion\n- The authors propose a unifying, mechanistic hypothesis that elevated intra-abdominal pressure in pregnancy (IAH-P) causes the clinical syndrome of pre-eclampsia via hemodynamic compromise, ischemia/reperfusion, intestinal permeability with LPS translocation, and resultant systemic inflammation; and that delivery relieves the condition by abdominal decompression.",
