{
  "url": "https://www.glowm.com/pdf/NEW-Pregnancy_Hypertension-chapter8.pdf",
  "title": "NEW-Pregnancy_Hypertension-Second_Revise.indb",
  "content": "Summary \u2014 Fluids, drugs and transfusion in hypertensive disorders of pregnancy (main details, key numbers, dosages, trial findings, conclusions)\n\nSynopsis / overarching points\n- Timed delivery of the placenta is the only cure for hypertensive disorders of pregnancy; management aims to optimize fetal outcome and reduce maternal end-organ complications.\n- Pre-eclampsia involves multisystem endothelial dysfunction; management includes: judicious fluid restriction, antihypertensive therapy (severe and non-severe), magnesium sulfate (MgSO4) for eclampsia prevention/treatment and fetal neuroprotection, antenatal corticosteroids (<34 weeks), transfusion and other therapies for HELLP. Most of these interventions (except fluid) are on the WHO Model List of Essential Medicines (2015).\n\nFluid management\n- Plasma volume expansion is NOT recommended for pre-eclampsia (4 trials, 277 women showed no improvement; largest trial n=216 associated with harm: more Caesareans, non-significant shorter prolongation, non-significant increase in pulmonary oedema).\n- IV fluid should be restricted to a maximum of 80 mL/h when an IV is in place in pre-eclampsia (NICE recommendation referenced).\n- Oliguria (<15 mL urine/h for 6 consecutive hours) common; avoid routine fluid bolus solely to increase urine output (risk of pulmonary oedema). Do not give furosemide unless pulmonary oedema or oliguric renal failure. \u201cRenal-dose\u201d dopamine not recommended.\n\nAntihypertensive therapy \u2014 general\n- Severe hypertension threshold: \u2265160/110 mmHg. Treat severe hypertension in pregnancy (WHO strong recommendation despite low-quality direct RCT evidence).\n- Goal of therapy: reduce blood pressure to a non-severe level (not necessarily normotension) to reduce stroke risk; monitor fetal heart rate (FHR) continuously until BP stable.\n- In hypertensive emergency aim to lower mean arterial pressure by \u226425% over minutes\u2013hours (example: 220/130 \u2192 165/98 mmHg over 1\u20132 h, then further to <160/100 over next 2 h).\n- Short-acting agents preferred for emergencies; consider arterial line if possible.\n\nAntihypertensive agents for severe hypertension \u2014 commonly recommended and dosing (from tables)\n- First-line options (hospital): short-acting oral nifedipine (capsule/tablet), IV labetalol, IV hydralazine.\n  - Nifedipine (capsule/tablet): 10 mg orally (swallow without biting) repeat every 30 min to max 30 mg (onset 5\u201310 min; peak ~30 min; duration \u22656 h depending on prep).\n  - Labetalol IV: start 20 mg over 2 min, then 40 mg, then 80 mg q30 min to max 300 mg; continuous infusion 1\u20132 mg/min (max 300 mg) if needed. Neonatal bradycardia risk \u2014 inform neonatology if in labour.\n  - Hydralazine IV: 5 mg bolus, repeat 5\u201310 mg q30 min up to 45 mg; may increase maternal hypotension and side effects.\n- Alternatives (supported less strongly): oral labetalol 200 mg, clonidine 0.1\u20130.2 mg (clonidine not recommended during breastfeeding), captopril 6.25\u201312.5 mg (postpartum only).\n- Refractory hypertension: sodium nitroprusside may be considered in ICU, but has monitoring/cyanide concerns; high-dose diazoxide associated with maternal hypotension.\n\nComparative trial evidence for severe hypertension\n- 47 trials, 4,322 women compared one short-acting agent vs another. Over half compared hydralazine, labetalol, nifedipine.\n- Hydralazine vs calcium channel blockers (usually nifedipine): hydralazine may be less effective and associated with more maternal side effects (11 trials, 699 women).\n- Hydralazine vs labetalol: hydralazine may be more effective at BP lowering but associated with more maternal hypotension and adverse effects (8 trials, 384 women). Parenteral labetalol associated with neonatal bradycardia in some trials.\n- Oral nifedipine vs parenteral labetalol: appears similarly effective in trials (e.g., 7 trials, 363 women); evidence supports nifedipine capsule use but cautions about dosing (10 mg increments recommended; avoid biting capsules/tablets unless specified).\n- MgSO4 is NOT an antihypertensive though can transiently lower BP in some patients; concurrent nifedipine + MgSO4 has neuromuscular blockade risk <1% (reversible with calcium gluconate).\n\nAntihypertensive therapy for non-severe hypertension (140\u2013159/90\u2013109 mmHg)\n- Antihypertensive therapy reduces risk of progression to transient severe hypertension: RR 0.49 (95% CI 0.40\u20130.60); 20 trials, 2,558 women; NNT = 10 (95% CI 8\u201313).\n- No clear differences in other maternal/perinatal outcomes in pooled trials (29 trials, 3,350 women).\n- CHIPS trial (Control of Hypertension In Pregnancy Study, NEJM 2015): \u201ctight\u201d control (target diastolic 85 mmHg) vs \u201cless tight\u201d (target diastolic 100 mmHg):\n  - Achieved BP difference: \u22125.8/\u22124.6 mmHg (p<0.001).\n  - Perinatal primary outcome (perinatal death or NICU >48 h): 30.7% vs 31.4% (aOR 0.98, 95% CI 0.74\u20131.30) \u2014 no difference.\n  - Birth weight <10th percentile: 19.7% vs 16.1% (aOR 1.28, 95% CI 0.93\u20131.79) \u2014 no clear difference.\n  - Severe maternal hypertension reduced: 27.5% vs 40.6% (aOR 0.56, 95% CI 0.42\u20130.75).\n  - Serious maternal complications similar: 2.0% vs 3.7% (aOR 0.57, 95% CI 0.26\u20131.27).\n- Recommendation from CHIPS: treat non-severe hypertension aiming for tighter control (diastolic ~85 mmHg) to reduce severe hypertension risk without clear harm to babies.\n\nAgents commonly used for non-severe hypertension\n- Methyldopa (250\u2013500 mg PO BID\u2013QID; max 2000 mg/day), labetalol (100\u2013400 mg PO BID\u2013TID; max 2400 mg/day), nifedipine (various formulations; PA/SR 10\u201320 mg PO BID\u2013TID or XL 20\u201360 mg OD\u2013BID). Methyldopa on WHO essential medicines list for pregnancy hypertension.\n- ACE inhibitors and ARBs should NOT be used in pregnancy (fetotoxic). Atenolol and prazosin discouraged (atenolol linked to fetal growth concerns; prazosin trial reported more stillbirths).\n\nLong-term paediatric neurodevelopment\n- Limited data; no consistent evidence that labetalol, nifedipine, methyldopa cause major adverse neurodevelopmental outcomes in RCT follow-ups (examples: nifedipine 1 yr n=110; atenolol 18 mo n=190; methyldopa 7.5 yr n=242).\n- Some observational data suggest modest/inconsistent increases in childhood neurodevelopment problems with gestational hypertension or pre-eclampsia (inattention, externalising behaviours, motor/verbal function).\n- Hypertensive disorders themselves linked to later offspring cardiovascular and possible cognitive effects.\n\nMagnesium sulphate (MgSO4) \u2014 treatment, prevention, fetal neuroprotection\n- MgSO4 on WHO Model List (2015) for treatment of eclampsia and severe pre-eclampsia.\n- Eclampsia treatment: MgSO4 more than halves recurrent seizures vs phenytoin (7 trials, 972 women), diazepam (7 trials, 1,396 women), or lytic cocktail (3 trials, 397 women). Typical regimen: loading 4 g IV over 5\u201310 min (5 g in some settings), then 1 g/h infusion; if seizure occurs on therapy give additional 2\u20134 g IV.\n- Eclampsia prevention in pre-eclampsia (Magpie Trial and others):\n  - MgSO4 vs placebo/no therapy: RR for eclampsia 0.41 (95% CI 0.29\u20130.58); 6 trials, 11,444 women.\n  - Magpie trial protocols: 4 g IV loading over 10\u201315 min, then 1 g/h infusion.\n  - NNT to prevent one seizure: severe pre-eclampsia NNT = 50 (95% CI 34\u2013100); non-severe pre-eclampsia NNT = 100 (95% CI 100\u2013500).\n  - MgSO4 decreased placental abruption risk (RR 0.64, 95% CI 0.50\u20130.83; NNT ~100) but increased Caesarean delivery (50% vs 47%; RR 1.05).\n- Comparative: MgSO4 superior to phenytoin for prevention; compared to nimodipine reduced eclampsia but increased respiratory problems and need for more antihypertensives in one large trial (n=1,650).\n- Cost-effectiveness and resource considerations:\n  - NNT to prevent one eclampsia: high-income countries = 324 (95% CI 122\u2013\u221e); low-income countries = 43 (95% CI 30\u201368).\n  - Incremental cost per eclampsia prevented (2001 US$): high-income \u2248 $21,202; low-income \u2248 $456. If restricted to severe pre-eclampsia: high-income $12,942; low-income $263.\n  - MgSO4 prophylaxis costly in HICs due to surveillance costs; more cost-effective in low-income settings.\n- MgSO4 for fetal neuroprotection:\n  - For imminent preterm birth <32+0 weeks: MgSO4 reduces cerebral palsy (RR 0.68, 95% CI 0.52\u20130.91) and combined death or CP (RR 0.86, 95% CI 0.74\u20131.00) \u2014 3 trials, 3,981 infants.\n  - Recommended for imminent delivery at \u226433+6 weeks for women with hypertensive disease.\n  - MgSO4 for fetal neuroprotection is cost-effective in preterm birth settings (examples: better QALYs and lower costs in certain analyses).\n- Dosing recommendations / practical points:\n  - First-line regimens:\n    - IV only: 4 g loading IV over 5 min then 1 g/h infusion.\n    - IV + IM (Pritchard): 4 g IV loading + 5 g IM each buttock (10 g IM total) then 5 g IM q4 h.\n    - If seizure occurs while on MgSO4, give additional 2\u20134 g IV over 5 min and increase infusion.\n  - Routine serum magnesium monitoring is not recommended.\n  - Data insufficient to endorse reduced-dose or abbreviated regimens for eclampsia prevention; some LMIC programs use modified regimens with variable evidence.\n  - MgSO4 is effective and safe when administered by trained providers; community administration trials and CLIP cluster trial underway (2013\u20132017) to assess community-level interventions (India, Nigeria, Mozambique, Pakistan).\n\nHELLP syndrome \u2014 laboratory course, transfusion, corticosteroids\n- Laboratory course: platelet nadir often postpartum (may worsen 0\u20132 days), improvement should begin by day 4; platelet count \u2265100\u00d710^9/L typically within 3 days of nadir.\n- Platelet transfusion thresholds (SOGC guidance summarized):\n  - Platelet count <20\u00d710^9/L: transfuse regardless of mode of delivery.\n  - Platelets 20\u201349\u00d710^9/L: transfuse prior to Caesarean delivery; consider for vaginal delivery if active bleeding, platelet dysfunction, rapidly falling count, or coagulopathy.\n  - Platelets \u226550\u00d710^9/L: consider transfusion if excessive active bleeding, platelet dysfunction, falling count or coagulopathy.\n  - Do not transfuse platelets if strong suspicion of HIT or TTP-HUS.\n- Practical: apheresis unit raises platelet count by at least ~5\u00d710^9/L (peak 10\u201360 min). Anti-D negative women receiving platelets should receive anti-D immunoglobulin (300 \u00b5g) to prevent sensitization.\n- Corticosteroids (dexamethasone 10 mg IV q12 h x48 h) speed laboratory improvement (11 trials, 550 women) but no proven reduction in major maternal or perinatal morbidity; routine use not recommended unless the faster laboratory improvement will change management (e.g., allow neuraxial anaesthesia or avoid platelet transfusion). COHELLP trial ongoing to assess maternal morbidity outcomes.\n- Recommend against routine plasma exchange/plasmapheresis for HELLP, particularly within first 4 days postpartum.\n\nThromboprophylaxis\n- Consider unfractionated or LMWH thromboprophylaxis if VTE risk \u22651%:\n  - Antenatally when pre-eclampsia plus \u22652 other risk markers.\n  - Postnatally when pre-eclampsia plus \u22651 other risk marker, or any hypertensive disorder with antenatal bedrest \u22657 days.\n- Guidelines based mainly on observational data.\n\nNovel and investigational therapies\n- Agents under investigation (insufficient evidence for routine use): pravastatin (STaMP trial), L-arginine, S-nitrosoglutathione (GSNO), sildenafil (STRIDER network for IUGR; sildenafil RCT for pre-eclampsia negative in late-onset disease), esomeprazole (PIE trial ongoing), recombinant antithrombin (PRESERVE-1).\n- Other interventions with limited/biased evidence: abdominal decompression (3 small trials suggested benefit but high bias), immediate postpartum curettage (some trials show physiologic improvements but not hard outcomes). Digibind and recombinant activated protein C have shown disappointing results.\n\nEvidence-based care in under-resourced settings (LMIC focus)\n- >99% of maternal deaths from hypertensive disorders occur in under-resourced settings (sub-Saharan Africa, South Asia).\n- Priority interventions (Table 8.5 summary):\n  - Community / primary care: detect, stabilize, and refer; give antihypertensives for severe HTN and MgSO4 before referral when indicated; clear communication with referral unit; consider antenatal corticosteroids individualized.\n  - Secondary-level facilities: appropriate use of antihypertensives, restricted fluids, MgSO4, corticosteroids; availability of packed RBCs.\n  - Tertiary-level facilities: same therapies plus availability of platelets and clotting factors; capacity for expectant management and advanced critical care (e.g., intra-arterial BP monitoring, ventilatory support).\n- Service-strengthening measures shown helpful: criteria-based clinical audit (improves outcomes when done well), emergency simulation drills (ESMOE/EOST), \u201ceclampsia box\u201d / standardized kits, monitoring/treatment charts, single-use emergency kits; Springfusor pump improved tolerability of IV MgSO4 in one trial (n=300).\n- Community initiatives: Perinatal Education Programme (PEP) for provider education; CLIP cluster RCT (2013\u20132017) testing community triage/treatment/transport with community health workers administering oral methyldopa and IM MgSO4.\n\nConsensus across international guidelines\n- Multiple international guidelines (WHO, NICE, SOGC, ACOG, NVOG, SOMANZ, QLD): consistent recommendations:\n  - Do NOT give plasma volume expansion in pre-eclampsia (SOGC, NICE, SOMANZ).\n  - Restrict IV fluids (NICE: \u226480 mL/h).\n  - Severe hypertension should be treated; most guidelines aim for <160/110 mmHg (some variation: NICE <150/80\u2013100 for non-severe targets, etc.).\n  - First-choice drugs: IV labetalol, oral nifedipine (short-acting), IV hydralazine (depending on guideline).\n  - MgSO4 indicated for eclampsia treatment and for prevention in severe pre-eclampsia; indicated for fetal neuroprotection when delivery imminent <34 weeks.\n  - ACE inhibitors/ARBs contraindicated in pregnancy.\n  - Corticosteroids not recommended to improve HELLP clinical outcomes (may be considered if platelet improvement changes management).\n\nBest practice / specific recommendations (selected, from text)\n- Fluids: no plasma expansion; IV fluid \u226480 mL/h; do not give fluids routinely for oliguria; avoid dopamine/furosemide for persistent oliguria.\n- Severe HTN: lower BP to <160/110 mmHg; first-line nifedipine short-acting, IV hydralazine, IV labetalol; MgSO4 is not an antihypertensive; continuous FHR monitoring until BP stable.\n- Non-severe HTN: aim diastolic BP ~85 mmHg; initial drug selection individualized (methyldopa, labetalol, nifedipine); avoid ACEi/ARBs; avoid atenolol/prazosin prior to delivery.\n- MgSO4: first-line for eclampsia treatment and prevention in severe pre-eclampsia; standard dosing (4 g IV loading then 1 g/h); consider MgSO4 for fetal neuroprotection if imminent preterm delivery \u226433+6 weeks.\n- HELLP: know local delays for platelet availability; transfuse platelets per thresholds above; do not routinely use corticosteroids for clinical benefit (unless management changed by labs); recommend against plasmapheresis early postpartum.\n\nKey trials / dates to note\n- Magpie Trial (Altman et al., Lancet 2002) \u2014 MgSO4 for eclampsia prevention (11,444 women included across trials).\n- CHIPS Trial (Magee et al., NEJM 2015) \u2014 tight vs less-tight BP control in non-severe hypertension.\n- WHO recommendations for prevention/treatment of pre-eclampsia/eclampsia (2011).\n- Ongoing/important trials: STaMP (pravastatin for early severe pre-eclampsia), PRESERVE-1 (recombinant antithrombin), STRIDER sildenafil trials for early-onset IUGR, CLIP cluster trial (2013\u20132017) for community interventions.\n\nPriorities for future research (selected)\n- Is nifedipine superior to parenteral agents for severe pregnancy hypertension?\n- Can MgSO4 be delivered more cost-effectively for eclampsia prevention (reduced dose or duration) without losing effectiveness?\n- Does dexamethasone reduce severe maternal morbidity in HELLP without increasing maternal risk?\n- Need more community-level research and interventions to reduce pre-hospital delays in triage, transport and treatment (many deaths occur before facility care).\n\nSelected quantitative findings (quick list)\n- Severe HTN definition: \u2265160/110 mmHg.\n- IV fluids in pre-eclampsia: limit to \u226480 mL/h.\n- MgSO4 regimens: 4 g IV loading then 1 g/h infusion; alternative IM doses described (Pritchard).\n- MgSO4 efficacy for prevention: RR for eclampsia 0.41 (95% CI 0.29\u20130.58); NNT severe = 50, non-severe = 100 (Magpie data).\n- MgSO4 fetal neuroprotection <32 wks: RR for cerebral palsy 0.68 (95% CI 0.52\u20130.91) (3 trials, 3,981 infants).\n- CHIPS effect on severe maternal HTN: 27.5% (tight) vs 40.6% (less-tight); aOR 0.56 (95% CI 0.42\u20130.75).\n- Cost per eclampsia prevented (2001 US$): HIC \u2248 $21,202; LIC \u2248 $456 (Magpie economic analysis).\n- Trials comparing short-acting agents: 47 trials, 4,322 women.\n\nThis summary preserves the principal recommendations, dosing regimens, threshold values, key trial results (including sample sizes, RRs, NNTs), cost-effectiveness findings, transfusion/corticosteroid thresholds for HELLP, and ongoing trial priorities as presented in the source.",
